# Two highly porous single-crystalline zirconium-based metal-organic frameworks

1Department of Chemistry, University of South Florida, Florida 33620, USA 2ChemMatCARS, Center for Advanced Radiation Sources, University of Chicago, Illinois 60439, USA
Herein we report two highly porous $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs, 1 and 2) constructed by the truncated octahedral secondary building unit (SBU) of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ and the organic linear ligand of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid $\operatorname{(H}_{2\mathbf{s}}\mathbf{b}\mathrm{dc)}$ or $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azobenezenedicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{abdc}}\right)$ . Both Zr-based MOFs are obtained as single crystals of suitable size for single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. Furthermore, these two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs have been fully characterized by powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) studies, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), infrared spectroscopy (IR) and gas adsorption analysis. In particular, their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas adsorption behaviors have been investigated and discussed.
## metal-organic frameworks, zirconium cluster, single-crystalline
Citation: Gao WY, Thiounn T, Wojtas L, Chen YS, Ma S. Two highly porous single-crystalline zirconium-based metal-organic frameworks. Sci China Chem, doi: 10.1007/s11426-016-0071-8
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [1], composed of polytopic organic ligands linking metal ions or clusters, have been emerging into an important class of porous solid-state materials at an exponential rate over the past two decades [2]. MOFs have thus captured unparalleled attention from both academia and industrial communities due to their structural regularity and synthetic tunability by successful use of crystal engineering and/or reticular chemistry strategies [3–5]. In principal, a structure-designated material can be targeted by judicious selection of the organic ligands and the inorganic secondary building units (SBUs) to match the vertex figures of a given net [6–8]. These intriguing modular features render MOFs to be a prominent platform for various applications in gas storage and separation [9,10], heterogeneous catalysis [11], sensing [12] and other areas [13–15].
Among a variety of inorganic SBUs encountered in
MOFs, the zirconium (IV) truncated octahedral SBU, $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ [16], and related expanded analogues [17] have recently been widely employed to construct a series of porous MOFs with high thermal and chemical stability [18–21]. However, it is still difficult to obtain single crystals of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs, which are large enough for single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction analysis, tentatively due to the fast formation of high-affinity $Z\mathrm{r}{-}\mathrm{O}$ bonds [22]. Herein, we report that two highly porous $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs (1 and 2) are constructed by the truncated octahedral SBU of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{12}$ and the organic linear ligand of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -stilbenedicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{sbdc}}\right)$ or $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azobenzenedicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{abdc}}\right)$ , both of which feature suitable size of single-crystals for single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction. During the synthesis of these two MOFs, acetic acid is indispensable as the modulator to tune the crystals’ size. Furthermore, a thorough characterization of these two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs has been conducted through powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) studies, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), infrared spectroscopy (IR) and gas adsorption analysis. In particular, their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas adsorption behaviors have been investigated and discussed.
Synthesis of 1. A mixture of $\operatorname{H}_{2\mathrm{sbdc}}$ ( $\mathrm{68.4~mg},$ 0.255 mmol), $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(60~\mathrm{mg},\ 0.255~\mathrm{mmol})$ and $N\mathrm{,}N^{\prime}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $4~\mathrm{mL}$ ) with acetic acid (HAc, $0.6~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added into a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ scintillation vial and then heated up to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . The resulting octahedron-shaped colorless crystals of 1 were obtained.
Synthesis of 2. Follow the similar reaction procedures with that of 1 except using $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ abdc (68.9 mg, 0.255 mmol) instead of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sbdc}$ . And the resulting octahedron-shaped orange crystals of 2 were obtained.
Complexes 1 and 2 were synthesized under very similar reaction conditions and relatively large octahedral single-crystals were harvested for single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction analysis. It is found that acetic acid modulates the MOF synthesis [22]. Acetic acid proved to be essential for the formation of single crystals. Otherwise, only fine powder samples would be obtained. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXD) analysis reveals that both 1 and 2 crystallize in the same cubic space group, $F m{-}3m$ , with empirical formula of $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(s b d c)_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(a b d c)_{6}}.$ , respectively. As illustrated in Figure 1, these two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs are built on the $Z\mathrm{r}(\mathrm{IV})$ truncated octahedral SBUs connected by the linear linkers of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sbdc and/or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{abdc}$ , which show the similar connectivity with the well-known UiO series. Basically, each $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based SBU is composed of an inner octahedral core, $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ , alternatively capped by $\mu_{3}–0$ and 4 $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ groups. All the octahedral edges are bridged by carboxylate groups from the linear linkers, generating a truncated octahedron (connecting the oxygen atoms from twelve carboxylate groups). The truncated octahedral SBUs are linked by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sbdc}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{abdc}$ , forming into the fcu-topology extended networks of 1 and 2. The structure of 1 and/or 2 contains two different types of polyhedral cage cavities: an octahedral cage that is face sharing with 8 tetrahedral cages and edge sharing with 8 additional octahedral cages, as shown in Figure S1 (Supporting Information online).
The phase purities of 1 and 2 were verified by PXRD studies, which indicate that the diffraction patterns of the fresh samples are consistent with the calculated ones (Figure S2 and Figure S3). TGA was performed on the fresh samples of 1 and 2 (Figure S4). A continuous weight loss of ${\sim}64\%$ from 25 to $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is observed in compound 1, which corresponds to the loss of guest solvent molecules trapped in the cavities. The plot is followed by a plateau at the range of 170 to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , before it falls into decomposition. Then the stepwise-like ligand decomposition ends at $560^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a weight loss of ${\sim}20\%$ . The compound 2 exhibits a similar weight loss process. But the ligand decomposition of 2 happens between 405 and $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Thus the substantial weight loss in the beginning indicates that these two MOFs demonstrate very high porosity, accommodating a large amount of guest molecules. Moreover, the two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based frameworks also show certain thermal stability. However, compared to UiO-66 [16], the less thermal stability of 1 and 2 can be tentatively attributed to the ligand extension from terephthalic acid to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{sbdc}$ or H2abdc.
To examine the permanent porosity of 1 and 2, gas adsorption studies were performed on the activated samples. The activation conditions are detailed in the Supporting Information. As shown in Figure 2(a) and (b), the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ for 1 and 2 are observed with uptake capacities of ${\sim}780$ and ${\sim}600~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, at the saturation pressure with typical type I adsorption behavior, which is a characteristic of microporous materials. Derived from the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption data, 1 possesses a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of ${\sim}2890~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ${(P/{P_{0}}{=}}0.0001{-}0.1$ ) and a corresponding Langmuir surface area of ${\sim}3237~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ $\scriptstyle(P/P_{0}=0.9)$ . Similarly, 2 shows a BET surface area of ${\sim}2122$ $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ $(P/P_{0}{=}0.0001{-}0.1)$ and a Langmuir surface area of ${\sim}2363~\mathrm{m^{2}/g}~(P/P_{0}{=}0.9)$ . Moreover, density functional theory (DFT) pore size distribution analysis based on the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption data at $77\mathrm{K}$ indicates that the pore sizes of 1 and 2 are both narrowly distributed at around $13.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is in good agreement with the channel width observed in crystal structures (Figure S5 and Figure S6).
Moreover, we evaluated the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake performances of 1 and 2. As shown in Figure 2(c), 1 can adsorb substantial amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with a capacity of $10.7~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(54.7~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $6.77~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(34.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 atm pressure. In comparison, 2 demonstrated a lower $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity with $8.83~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(44.9\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ at $273\mathrm{K}$ and $5.20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $(26.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ under 1 atm pressure, as illustrated in Figure 2(d). Despite the fact that both $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs are characterized by high surface areas, the values of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity under low pressure are very moderate compared to other porous MOFs with open metal centers or Lewis base sites. This can be attributed to the absence of functional sites interacting with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, which is explained by the relatively low heats of adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (21.7 and $18.8\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at the initial range, Figure S10) calculated for 1 and 2.
In summary, two highly porous single-crystalline $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs with fcu topology, 1 and 2, have been built based upon the truncated octahedral SBU of $\mathrm{{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{{O}_{4}(O H)_{4}(C O_{2})_{12}}}$ and the linear ligand of $\operatorname{H}_{2\mathrm{{sbdc}}}$ and H2abdc. Large single-crystals have been obtained by using the modulator of acetic acid. A thorough characterization of these two $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs has been conducted by virtue of SCXD, PXRD, TGA, FT-IR and gas adsorption analysis. In particular, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas adsorption behaviors have been described and tentatively interpreted.
